// The Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W, reached through the C SDK -- a package, in the sense
// `design/packages.md` gives the word.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because this depends on nothing that resolves: what it needs is
// the pico-sdk, and the pico-sdk is not a sysl package and could not be one. It is a CMake build
// system that generates a second-stage bootloader, selects a board header and drives the final link
// with its own linker script -- none of which fits inside a thing `sysl build` fetches and compiles.
//
// So the arrangement is the other way up. **The SDK's CMake owns the link, and a program using this
// package is built with `sysl build-c` into an archive that CMake then links.** That is the one
// unusual thing about this package and everything else follows from it.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what arrived
// against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       pico2 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/pico2", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.0` on this repository. The coordinate names the *package*, `pico2`, while the module a
// program imports is `sh.sysl.pico2`; `packages.md § 9` keeps those two deliberately apart.

package {
  name    = "pico2"
  version = "0.1.0"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block.** This asks for no allocator and no operating system,
// because a microcontroller has neither -- every name here is a call into C that the SDK's own link
// satisfies, and a call into C is not a capability.
//
// **What it also does not do is carry a `@link` directive**, and the reason is different from every
// other package in the org. termbox2 has none because its implementation is vendored and compiled
// here; qcbor names `m` because it reaches `pow`. This one has none because the symbols arrive from
// a link line that CMake writes and sysl never sees. The absence looks the same and means something
// else, which is worth knowing when comparing the two.
